{"title":"Reporting Your Findings","markdown":{"headingText":"Reporting Your Findings","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-reporting","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nThe reporting section represents the final stage of the analytical process, where statistical findings are translated into clear, structured, and interpretable statements. The objective is not to restate outputs generated by software, but to communicate results in a manner that is scientifically accurate, concise, and aligned with the research question. Effective reporting requires selection, synthesis, and interpretation of relevant results, rather than indiscriminate presentation of all computed statistics.\n\nA well-constructed report integrates numerical results with narrative explanation. Key elements include point estimates, measures of variability, effect sizes, and corresponding confidence intervals, supplemented where appropriate by p-values. The emphasis should be on magnitude and direction of effects rather than sole reliance on statistical significance. Tables and figures should be used strategically to present complex information efficiently, avoiding redundancy between text and visuals. Each reported result must directly address a predefined objective or hypothesis.\n\nStandardization is critical in reporting. Established guidelines such as CONSORT for randomized trials and STROBE for observational studies provide structured frameworks to ensure completeness and transparency. Adherence to such guidelines improves reproducibility, facilitates peer review, and enhances the credibility of findings.\n\nWithin Jamovi, outputs are often presented in tabular formats that require refinement before inclusion in reports. This includes appropriate rounding, labeling, ordering of variables, and alignment with journal or thesis requirements. The reporting section should ultimately enable a reader to understand what was analyzed, what was found, and how those findings should be interpreted, without needing to refer back to raw output or software-specific details.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"monashthesis-html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"monashthesis-html","base-format":"html","extension-name":"monashthesis"},"execute":{"fig-width":8,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":false,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":true,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":["_extensions/monashthesis/fonts.html"],"notebook-links":true,"shortcodes":[]},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","toc-depth":2,"number-sections":true,"html-math-method":"katex","include-in-header":{"file":"_extensions/monashthesis/fonts.html"},"filters":[],"toc":true,"output-file":"reporting.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links"